#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073447 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073738 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f3ac1e54-c949-5ffe-b377-b0e35240a13c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equivalent Ratios"}] :question/id #uuid "a453e80d-016f-5a6c-9890-705463eb1fb9" :question/math-academy-id "e-13120" :question/problem "Which of the following ratios is equivalent to $12: 9$?\n\n1. $2: 3$\n2. $4: 3$\n3. $5: 3$" :question/worked-solution "All of the answer choices have the second number equal to $3$. So our equivalent ratio is of the form $0$\n\nTo find a ratio that's equivalent to $12: 9$, we divide both numbers by the same value. In particular, we need to get $3$ from ${\\color{red}{9}}$, so we divide ${\\color{red}{9}}$ by $3$.\n\n$$\n12\\begin{vmatrix}: & 9 \\\\ ↓ \\div 3 & ↓ \\div 3 \\\\ [math]\\phantom{0}[/math] &:\\end{vmatrix}3\n$$\n\nSo to get $0$ from ${\\color{blue}{12}}$, we divide $12$ by $3$ as well:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}12} \\div 3 =4\n$$\n\nTherefore, $12:9$ is equivalent to $4:3$.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073448 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073739 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ae17eb3f-f444-5ab3-82fd-6c92b97b0e41" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Ratios"}] :question/id #uuid "0a57949c-efc9-5403-9e7b-08346153f184" :question/math-academy-id "e-13121" :question/problem "![](images/f3/f37699e58adadd6450ebc73bc969606b6f24dc8a0a52fd206ad116b2c6703a88.png)\n\nWhat ratio describes the number of triangles to the total number of shapes? Give your answer in its simplest form." :question/worked-solution "First, we count the number of triangles. We see that there are $4$ triangles.\n\nThen, we count the total number of shapes. We see that there are $6$ shapes in total.\n\nSo the ratio of triangles to total shapes is $4:6$.\n\nWe can simplify this ratio by dividing both numbers in the ratio by $2$:\n\n$$\n4\\begin{vmatrix}: & 6 \\\\ ↓ \\div 2 & ↓ \\div 2 \\\\ 2 &:\\end{vmatrix}3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ratio of triangles to the total shapes is $2:3$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073449 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073740 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d16202af-d3ac-54bc-9c76-25947a7a0b60" :knowledge-point/title "Completing a Statement"}] :question/id #uuid "dd86c5a5-b8be-5d19-a477-dcf46683881f" :question/math-academy-id "e-13123" :question/problem "![](images/8d/8d9f0a004ca9beb84b3a2a604d71dbf43ab70a67d899b167c5183ecc2ebe15f3.png)\n\nWhat number is missing from the boxes in the statement below?\n\n> The ratio of squares to circles is $0$ because there is $1$ square for every $0$ circles." :question/worked-solution "First, we count the number of squares. We see that there are $2$ squares.\n\nThen, we count the number of circles. We see that there are $2$ circles.\n\nSo the ratio of circles to squares is $2:2$.\n\nWe can simplify this ratio by dividing both numbers by $2$:\n\n$$\n2\\begin{vmatrix}: & 2 \\\\ ↓ \\div 2 & ↓ \\div 2 \\\\ 1 &:\\end{vmatrix}1\n$$\n\nThis means that $2:2$ is equivalent to $1:1$.\n\nSo, the complete statement is\n\"The ratio of squares to circles is $1:\\bbox[3px,lightgray]{1}\\,$ because there is $1$ square for every $\\bbox[3px,lightgray]{1}\\,$circle.\""}]}
